<p>NASA’s Artemis II crew flew farther from Earth than any humans in history during a lunar flyby, breaking the Apollo 13 record of 248,655 miles set in 1970. The four-person crew — NASA astronauts Reid Wiseman, Victor Glover and Christina Koch, plus Canadian Space Agency astronaut Jeremy Hansen — reached about 252,756-252,757 miles from Earth before starting the trip home, on the first crewed voyage to the Moon’s vicinity since the Apollo era. During the flyby, Orion came within about 4,070 miles of the lunar surface and passed behind the Moon, triggering a planned communications blackout of roughly 40 minutes before contact resumed. The mission is a crewed test flight for NASA’s Artemis program, which aims to return astronauts to the Moon and establish a longer-term human presence there before applying that experience to future Mars missions. Several reports also described the flight as a lift for science and public morale during a difficult stretch of research funding cuts and project cancellations at NASA. President Trump congratulated the astronauts as the spacecraft headed back toward Earth, telling them they had made history.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/cmgWSyYviZ9c9l9Vyt0qA7713yfH_yItM4EGwSvhbtw5L_P7lYTvL8-DGNqjw9qp4sGCu6m4kdtV12pYupj3tKfQesOxiZLP7tPFtt27TcfQ' alt='This screengrab from a NASA livestream shows the Orion spacecraft approaching the Moon on April 6.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Far side science: Astronauts reported seeing meteor &#x27;impact flashes&#x27; on the Moon’s shadowed far side, giving humans a direct view of activity in a region normally hidden from Earth.</li><li>Crew milestone: Artemis II includes the first woman, Christina Koch, and the first Black astronaut, Victor Glover, ever assigned to a lunar mission.</li><li>Tribute to Apollo: After passing the old mark, one Artemis II crew member saluted earlier generations of explorers.</li><li>Extended observation window: The lunar flyby gave the crew about six hours to survey and photograph the Moon’s far side.</li><li>Far-side eclipse: NASA said the astronauts experienced their own solar eclipse as Orion moved behind the Moon during the flyby.</li></ul><blockquote>You’ve made history - President Donald Trump</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>NASA: NASA said the far-side signal loss was a planned part of the flyby and that the crew would keep working through it, using the blackout to photograph and observe the lunar far side until communications resumed. (<a href='https://www.rt.com/news/637515-artemis-moon-mission-flyby/'>RT</a>)</li><li>President Trump: President Trump praised the crew in a live exchange, asked about the Moon’s far side and the communications blackout, and congratulated the astronauts on making history. (<a href='https://www.thehindu.com/sci-tech/science/donald-trump-praises-artemis-astronauts-youve-made-history/article70832792.ece'>The Hindu</a>)</li><li>Science observers: Scientists at NASA’s Johnson Space Center used the crew’s real-time reports of lunar impact flashes to log fresh observations from the Moon’s darkened hemisphere. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1989403/artemis-ii-moon-crew-flies-farther-than-humans-have-ever-gone-before'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>Straits Times analysis: The mission was described as a morale boost for NASA after a year of budget cuts, shuttered projects, and retrenchments, underscoring its importance beyond the flight itself. (<a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/united-states/artemis-ii-nasa-carries-out-morale-boosting-moon-mission-during-tough-year-for-science'>The Straits Times</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1989403/artemis-ii-moon-crew-flies-farther-than-humans-have-ever-gone-before'>Artemis II moon crew flies farther than humans have ever gone before</a> - dawn.com</li><li><a href='https://www.thehindu.com/sci-tech/science/donald-trump-praises-artemis-astronauts-youve-made-history/article70832792.ece'>Donald Trump pra